+# Decision: Delivery transport for committed board revisions
+
+## Context
+
+Editors submit commands over HTTPS and the server assigns each committed edit one authoritative event revision. Separately, up to 800 browsers per event need to learn about that revision so the board they display stays current. How that revision reaches connected browsers is a transport choice that the change store, delivery, and browser-sync components are all sized around: the change store's revision/outbox shape, delivery's session model, and the browser's reconnect logic all follow from it.
+
+A busy market runs 10-25 editors producing about two status changes per minute on average, with bursts to 20 edits per second around opening time. 800 browsers may be watching simultaneously. The board must show committed changes within 15 seconds under normal connectivity and recover a browser that was disconnected for up to 20 minutes. Getting this transport wrong means living with either sluggish bursts, an unpredictable infrastructure bill, or in-house connection-handling code for the life of the project.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Gate — meets the 15-second freshness goal.** Inherited from the HLD's goals. Any option that cannot reliably deliver a committed revision to a connected browser within 15 seconds under normal connectivity is eliminated outright.
+- **Criterion — latency under burst (major).** Opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second are routine, not an edge case; staff coordinating a live market feel added lag directly.
+- **Criterion — recurring operating cost (moderate).** Forty markets running this board indefinitely makes a per-event or per-connection vendor fee a standing line item, not a one-time cost.
+- **Criterion — vendor coupling (moderate).** Tied to the tenet *self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted* — the project is free to choose either, so coupling is weighed on its own merits rather than ruled out or in by policy.
+- **Criterion — operational burden owned by the team (major).** Tied to the tenet *connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board* — whichever option is chosen, someone has to build and maintain the mechanism that keeps a dropped connection from silently looking like a quiet board. This criterion asks how much of that mechanism the team must build and run itself versus get from a vendor.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Browsers issue a cursor-based HTTPS request every five seconds asking for revisions after their last-seen cursor. The server answers from the change store's revision log; no persistent connection is held. This is the simplest option operationally: delivery stays plain request/response, and a dropped browser simply resumes polling with its last cursor once connectivity returns, which also happens to double as reconnect-after-disconnect handling.
+
+- Freshness gate: **passes**. A five-second poll interval delivers well inside the 15-second goal under normal connectivity.
+- Latency under burst: **weak**. Every browser re-reads the board every five seconds regardless of whether anything changed, so 800 browsers generate a constant read load on the change store, and a change can sit for up to five seconds before the next poll picks it up — worse under the exact bursts where freshness matters most.
+- Recurring operating cost: **strong**. No persistent connections, no vendor subscription; cost scales with ordinary HTTPS request volume.
+- Vendor coupling: **strong**. No realtime vendor at all.
+- Operational burden owned by the team: **strong**. Nothing beyond a cursor query the change store already needs to support and a standard HTTPS server.
+- Summary: simplest and cheapest, at the cost of structurally repeated reads and up to several seconds of built-in lag.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+The server holds an open WebSocket per connected browser and pushes each committed revision to subscribed sessions as it commits. The change store needs a replayable revision log so a reconnecting session can request everything it missed; delivery owns the socket session lifecycle (accept, authenticate, track subscriptions, detect drops); browsers implement reconnect-with-backoff and a catch-up request against the last revision they saw.
+
+- Freshness gate: **passes**. Push delivery reaches browsers as soon as a revision commits, well inside 15 seconds.
+- Latency under burst: **strong**. A push model has no polling interval to wait out; 800 browsers impose connection-count load rather than read load, and bursts are delivered as fast as the socket layer can fan them out.
+- Recurring operating cost: **strong**. No per-message or per-connection vendor fee; cost is the compute and connection capacity the team already provisions.
+- Vendor coupling: **strong**. No realtime vendor; the board's delivery path depends on nothing outside infrastructure the team already controls.
+- Operational burden owned by the team: **weak**. The team owns connection-capacity planning, reconnect-with-backoff, session authentication, and catch-up-after-disconnect — none of which the HTTPS command path needed before.
+- Summary: fastest and cheapest on a recurring basis, paid for with connection and reconnect machinery the team must build and keep working.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+A managed pub/sub or realtime vendor holds the browser connections; the server publishes each committed revision to the vendor, which fans it out to subscribed browsers. Delivery becomes a thin wrapper around the provider's publish API; the change store needs publication reconciliation (confirming a committed revision was actually handed to the provider); browsers use the provider's own client library and subscription/reconnect model.
+
+- Freshness gate: **passes**. Hosted realtime providers are built for sub-second fan-out, well inside 15 seconds.
+- Latency under burst: **strong**. Providers in this category are built to absorb exactly this kind of fan-out burst.
+- Recurring operating cost: **weak**. Pricing scales with connections and messages; 800 browsers across 40 markets with opening-time bursts is a standing, usage-correlated bill rather than a fixed infrastructure cost.
+- Vendor coupling: **weak**. Delivery, the change store's publication step, and the browser's subscription code all take on a direct dependency on one provider's API and availability.
+- Operational burden owned by the team: **strong**. Connection capacity, reconnect handling, and session scaling are the provider's problem; the team owns publication reconciliation and the provider integration, not the connection layer itself.
+- Summary: least connection-ops work and the fastest path to production, paid for with a recurring bill and a vendor dependency sitting on the board's critical path.
+
+## Selection
+
+Self-hosted WebSockets wins. It is the only option that is **strong** on both major criteria — latency under burst and avoiding a vendor on the critical path — while polling loses on burst latency (the exact condition the project is optimizing for, given opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second) and the hosted service loses on recurring cost and vendor coupling. The deciding tradeoff is operational: this option scores **weak** on operational burden, meaning the team is accepting connection-capacity planning and reconnect/catch-up logic as work it owns indefinitely, in exchange for push-speed updates with no recurring vendor bill.
+
+This turns on the tenet *self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted* — the project treats owning infrastructure as a legitimate choice rather than a fallback, so a vendor's lower ops burden does not automatically win. It also turns on *connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board*: because this option makes the team responsible for reconnect and catch-up, that tenet becomes an obligation on the delivery and browser-sync components specifically, not something a vendor's client library can be assumed to already guarantee.
+
+Committing to this forecloses designing the change store around a simple poll-friendly cursor query and forecloses treating delivery as a thin provider wrapper. It obligates the change store to a replayable, reliably published revision log; delivery to own socket session lifecycle end to end; and browser sync to implement reconnect-with-backoff and catch-up against the last seen revision — all three downstream nodes are sized around this choice.
